talk(441,'1978-03-20','V. M. Buchstaber, with A. V. Shokurov','P000772-P000773','Landweber-Novikov operations and formal substitutions',r'''The lecture identifies the Landweber--Novikov algebra with invariant differential operators on the formal substitution group, translates stably complex bordism into genus polynomials, and states a Hattori--Stong integrality criterion using the Todd translate of integral substitutions. It ends with the genuine question of computing cohomology of the integral substitution group. That question requires separating finite-jet, continuous, and abstract group cohomology; later real finite-jet results and the chapter's bounded integral calculation do not determine all of these theories.''')
item(441,'P000772','definition','Formal substitutions, jets, and the positive Witt algebra',r'''\(\operatorname{Diff}_1\) consists of \(\varphi(t)=t+a_1t^2+a_2t^3+\cdots\), with \(\varphi(0)=0\), \(\varphi'(0)=1\), and is the inverse limit of its jet groups. Its Lie algebra is denoted \(L_1\), and polynomial functions in the coefficients form \(P\).''',r'''Over a characteristic-zero field, composition gives a pro-unipotent group of tangent-to-identity formal substitutions. Its complete Lie algebra is \(t^2\mathbb R[[t]]\partial_t\); the graded polynomial subalgebra has basis \(e_i=t^{i+1}\partial_t\), \(i\ge1\), and \([e_i,e_j]=(j-i)e_{i+j}\). Polynomial coordinate functions depend on finitely many jets.''','A',r'''The group and coefficient Hopf algebra are the framework of \citet[Section~1]{buchstabershokurov1978landweber}, submitted 5 May 1977 and published 1978.''',['buchstabershokurov1978landweber'],match='exact',literal='correct',reconstruction='The distinction between the complete formal Lie algebra and its graded polynomial part is explicit; cochains later require the corresponding continuous or graded convention.')
item(441,'P000772-P000773','construction','A stably complex manifold as a genus polynomial',r'''Put \(\psi(t)=t/\varphi(t)\); take the degree-\(n\) coefficient of \(\prod_j\psi(\beta_jt)\), express it in elementary symmetric functions, substitute Chern classes, and pair with \(M\).''',r'''For a closed stably complex manifold of real dimension \(2n\), evaluate the multiplicative characteristic sequence with power series \(t/\varphi(t)\) on its stable tangent Chern roots. The degree-\(2n\) component paired with the fundamental class is a polynomial in \(a_1,\ldots,a_n\). Stabilization adds zero roots and leaves the value unchanged.''','A',r'''The genus-polynomial realization underlies \citet[Sections~1--2]{buchstabershokurov1978landweber}; its manuscript predates this lecture.''',['buchstabershokurov1978landweber'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Closedness, orientation, tangent-bundle convention, and cohomological degree are supplied.')
item(441,'P000773','construction','Geometric characteristic operations and their scope',r'''Products of manifolds and cycles dual to Chern polynomials illustrate operations. A first Chern class is represented by zeros of a section; Landweber--Novikov operations are then called all natural cohomological operations.''',r'''A transverse section of a complex line bundle gives a codimension-two submanifold dual to its first Chern class. General characteristic operations in complex bordism require the bordism construction and stable normal data; an arbitrary Chern polynomial does not canonically produce a single embedded manifold. The Landweber--Novikov algebra is the operation subalgebra \(S\); the full stable-operation algebra also involves coefficient operations.''','A',r'''\citet[Theorem~1 and surrounding definitions]{buchstabershokurov1978landweber} identifies the precise algebra \(S\). It does not justify an unrestricted assertion covering every natural operation in every cohomology theory.''',['buchstabershokurov1978landweber'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The distinction between geometric examples, S, and the entire operation algebra is essential. The notebook word canonical does not determine whether its bundle is the canonical line bundle or a tautological characteristic bundle.')
item(441,'P000773','theorem','Operations as left-invariant differential operators',r'''Theorem~1 associates \(c_\omega:M\mapsto M_\omega\) with a left-invariant differential operator \(D_\omega\) on genus polynomials, asserting a bijective correspondence.''',r'''The integral Landweber--Novikov algebra is identified with the appropriately integral left-invariant differential-operator algebra on the formal substitution group. After tensoring with \(\mathbb Q\), it is the universal enveloping algebra of the positive graded vector-field Lie algebra. The integral structure is not the naive enveloping algebra over \(\mathbb Z\); divided-power phenomena matter.''','A',r'''Exact match to \citet[Theorem~1 and its corollary]{buchstabershokurov1978landweber}, received May 1977, published 1978.''',['buchstabershokurov1978landweber'],match='exact',reconstruction='The rational versus integral qualification prevents replacing the operation lattice by an incorrect integral enveloping algebra.')
item(441,'P000773','theorem','The Todd translate and bordism integrality',r'''A polynomial comes from a manifold precisely when it takes integral values on the right coset of \(\varphi_T(t)=1-e^{-t}\) by \(\operatorname{Diff}_1(\mathbb Z)\); the theorem is compared with Stong--Hattori.''',r'''Within the graded rational genus-polynomial realization of complex bordism used in the paper, the bordism lattice is characterized by integrality on the Todd translate \(\varphi_T\operatorname{Diff}_1(\mathbb Z)\), with composition in the stated order. This is a statement about the identified characteristic-number space, not about all arbitrary functions on the substitution group.''','A',r'''\citet[Theorem~2]{buchstabershokurov1978landweber} gives precisely the arithmetic characterization and relates it to the Hattori--Stong theorem. Submission preceded the lecture; publication was in 1978.''',['buchstabershokurov1978landweber'],match='exact',reconstruction='The graded polynomial domain and the right-coset composition convention are part of the theorem.')
item(441,'P000773','theorem','Rational Ext and vector-field cohomology',r'''\(\operatorname{Ext}_S^*(\mathbb Q,\mathbb Q)=H^*(L_1)\). The notes also say that all homotopy invariants of continuous maps are expressed through this cohomology.''',r'''Under the rational enveloping-algebra identification, \(\operatorname{Ext}_{S\otimes\mathbb Q}^*(\mathbb Q,\mathbb Q)\cong H^*_{\rm CE}(L_1;\mathbb Q)\), with the internal grading retained. Complex-bordism operations enter Adams--Novikov spectral sequences, but their rational Ext with trivial coefficients is not by itself a classification of all continuous maps or all stable homotopy groups.''','A',r'''The Ext comparison follows from \citet[Theorem~1 and cohomological consequences]{buchstabershokurov1978landweber}. The broad sentence about all invariants is motivational shorthand and has no exact theorem identified in this form.''',['buchstabershokurov1978landweber'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',reconstruction='Rationalization, augmentation coefficients, and the restricted role of the spectral sequence are made explicit.')
item(441,'P000773','problem','Cohomology of formal substitutions with integer coefficients',r'''\ru{Задача: вычислить когомологии группы} \(\operatorname{Diff}_1(\mathbb Z)\). The immediately preceding definition specifies integral coefficients of a tangent-to-identity formal series. No coefficient module or cochain topology is stated.''',r'''Let \(J=\{t+a_1t^2+a_2t^3+\cdots:a_i\in\mathbb Z\}\) under composition. A precise finite-jet version is \(H^*_{\rm jet}(J;A)=\varinjlim_NH^*(J/(t^{N+1});A)\) with a trivial discrete abelian coefficient group and inflation maps. This is distinct from unrestricted cohomology of the abstract group \(J\). The full integral finite-jet cohomology ring is a natural precise refinement; the original wording does not choose between these theories.''','C',r'''\citet[Theorem~1.5]{babenkobogatyi2008substitutions} computes real cohomology with uniformly locally constant supports as the cohomology of \(L_1\), resolving that natural real version. Their Theorem~1.2 computes the continuous abelianization \(\mathbb Z^2\oplus(\mathbb Z/2)^2\). \citet{pavezcornejo2025abelianization}, first circulated in 2024, also uses the closure of the commutator subgroup; neither statement identifies the abstract discrete abelianization.''',['babenkobogatyi2008substitutions','pavezcornejo2025abelianization'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',match='partial',problem='GP-1977-40',worth='Worth developing in a specified cochain category: the real theory is known, while integral torsion and multiplication require additional information that real coefficients discard.',development='developed in this chapter',reconstruction='The integral finite-jet formulation is an explicit refinement, not a claim about the unstated original coefficients. The chapter proves bounded consequences of known abelianization, without asserting a full solution or novelty.',status=r'''As of 10 November 2026: the real finite-jet version is known. The chapter proves \(\operatorname{tors}H^2_{\rm jet}(J;\mathbb Z)\cong(\mathbb Z/2)^2\) and that the cup product of the two integral degree-one generators vanishes, by an explicit four-jet coboundary. The full integral ring and unrestricted abstract group cohomology are not determined by these results; no unsupported assertion that either is wholly unstudied is made.''')
problem('GP-1977-40','Cohomology of the integral formal substitution group',r'''Compute the cohomology of \(J=\{t+\sum_{i\ge1}a_it^{i+1}:a_i\in\mathbb Z\}\). The notebook leaves coefficients and topology unstated. This chapter distinguishes finite-jet cohomology \(\varinjlim_NH^*(J/(t^{N+1});A)\), continuous cochains, and unrestricted cohomology of the abstract group.''',['1977-441.7'],r'''As of November 2026, Babenko--Bogatyi's real uniformly locally constant-support theory is computed and its continuous abelianization is known. For the explicit integral finite-jet refinement, the chapter derives torsion \((\mathbb Z/2)^2\) in degree two and a vanishing product of the two integral degree-one generators. It does not compute the full integral ring or the abstract discrete theory. Pavez-Cornejo's 2024 preprint/2025 publication uses closed commutators, not ordinary abstract commutators.''',['babenkobogatyi2008substitutions','pavezcornejo2025abelianization'])
correction('P000773','all natural operations','Landweber-Novikov operations are all natural cohomological operations','specify the Landweber-Novikov subalgebra S and the cohomology theory','The full complex-bordism stable operation algebra also contains coefficient operations; the opened1978 paper states the precise S identification.')
save()

talk(442,'1978-03-20','F. Calogero and A. Degasperis','P000774-P000776','Spectral evolution, conserved quantities, and an adjacent talk on caustics',r'''The main lecture studies KdV recursion operators and their nonisospectral extensions, linear evolution of scattering data, and trace-type conserved quantities. Several normalization claims fail elementary low-order checks. The bottom of P000776 explicitly starts a different lecture: V. P. Palamodov at the Mathematical Society, 21 March 1978, on lacunas, sharp fronts, and singularity deformations. That adjacent material is retained with its actual speaker. P000777 starts the next Calogero lecture.''')
item(442,'P000774','construction','The recursion operator and the nonisospectral hierarchy',r'''\(Lf=f_{xx}-4uf+2u_x\int_x^\infty f\), and \(u_t=\alpha(L,t)u_x+\beta(L,t)(xu_x+2u)\). For \(\alpha=-L,\beta=0\) this is KdV; powers of \(L\) give the higher hierarchy.''',r'''For smooth rapidly decaying \(u\), with \(\partial^{-1}f=-\int_x^\infty f\), \(L=\partial^2-4u-2u_x\partial^{-1}\). Polynomial \(\alpha,\beta\) define the indicated hierarchy on a domain stable under these operations; more general functions require functional calculus. Since \(Lu_x=u_{xxx}-6uu_x\), the stated sign gives \(u_t=6uu_x-u_{xxx}\).''','A',r'''The nonisospectral extension was submitted 9 January 1978 and published in May: \citet{calogerodegasperis1978extension}. Its publisher record points to a June 1977 Rome lecture precursor. The exact operator-level identity here is also checked directly from the supplied formula.''',['calogerodegasperis1978extension'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='Decay, the inverse-derivative convention, and polynomial rather than arbitrary undefined functional calculus are supplied.')
item(442,'P000774','method','The Fourier analogy and the nonlinear spectral transform',r'''A constant-coefficient linear equation becomes an ordinary differential equation after Fourier transform; an extra term linear in \(x\) becomes differentiation in the spectral variable. The same pattern motivates the scattering transform.''',r'''Fourier transformation turns \(\partial_x\) into multiplication by \(ik\) and \(x\) into \(i\partial_k\), on a suitable function space. For the one-dimensional linear Schr\"odinger spectral problem, the scattering map depends nonlinearly on the potential; it is not the ordinary Fourier transform of \(u\); its admissible inverse data and reconstruction equation must be retained.''','E',r'''The analogy and Wronskian method were already used by \citet[abstract]{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. The page's inserted ``linear'' is essential to the scope of the Fourier sentence.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial')
item(442,'P000774-P000775','construction','Scattering data and their linear transport',r'''\(u\leftrightarrow\{R(k,t);p_n,c_n^2\}\), with inverse reconstruction by Gelfand--Levitan. \(R_t=2ik\alpha(-4k^2,t)R-k\beta(-4k^2,t)R_k\); \(p_t=p\beta(-4p^2,t)\); the norming-constant equation is omitted.''',r'''For a real sufficiently decaying potential, the complete one-dimensional scattering data consist of a reflection coefficient, negative eigenvalues, and norming constants. The displayed first-order transport equation is the proposed continuous-data evolution. If a bound state is parametrized by \(k=i\kappa\), its argument \(-4k^2\) is \(+4\kappa^2\); one must know whether the notebook's \(p\) is a complex spectral point or a positive decay constant before applying its last formula.''','A',r'''\citet{calogerodegasperis1978extension} is the closely matching nonisospectral publication; publisher metadata and editorial footnotes, not the full normalization proof, were obtained. The omitted norming equation prevents a complete algorithm from this page alone.''',['calogerodegasperis1978extension'],reconstruction='The literal p/rho ambiguity is preserved. The statement about changing soliton amplitudes and velocities is appropriate for nonisospectral motion, subject to the chosen real or complex flow.',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',confidence='medium')
item(442,'P000774-P000775','theorem','Conserved moments from the scaling seed',r'''\(c^{(n)}=\int_{\mathbb R}L^n(xu_x+2u)\,dx\), and \(\beta=0\) implies \(\dot c^{(n)}=0\).''',r'''For an isospectral KdV-hierarchy flow with sufficiently smooth rapid decay, these recursion expressions represent trace invariants, up to the chosen normalization. The first two are \(c^{(0)}=\int u\,dx\) and \(c^{(1)}=-3\int u^2\,dx\), obtained by integration by parts. Boundary terms must vanish.''','A',r'''\citet{calogerodegasperis1978conservation}, published June 1978, explicitly studies conserved quantities for these old and new spectral-transform classes. The abstract supports the identification; a complete formula-by-formula publication match is not claimed.''',['calogerodegasperis1978conservation'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The two low-order identities are direct checks of the notebook normalization, not a claim to have read its complete hierarchy in the later paper.')
item(442,'P000775','construction','Hamiltonian operators and the order of recursion factors',r'''\(I_0=\int u^2dx\), \(u_t=A_n\delta I_0/\delta u\), \(A_n=L^n\partial\). Novikov then writes \(L=\partial^{-1}L_3\), with inverse differentiation on the left.''',r'''For the convention on P000774, \(J_1=\partial\), \(J_2=\partial^3-2(u\partial+\partial u)\), and the recursion on evolutionary vector fields is \(L=J_2J_1^{-1}\). The operator \(J_1^{-1}J_2\) instead acts on variational gradients. These factor orders cannot be identified on the same space. Also \(\delta I_0/\delta u=2u\), introducing a factor two in the time normalization.''','E',r'''The Hamiltonian spectral framework is pre-existing: \citet{zakharovfaddeev1971kdv}. The earlier occurrence index includes the recursive-hierarchy problem GP-1973-53; this lecture uses the recursion and is not a new occurrence of an unresolved classification problem.''',['zakharovfaddeev1971kdv'],literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The vector-field/gradient distinction explains the two legitimate orders. Higher nonlocal Poisson brackets require a specified domain and inverse derivative; the notes suppress these.')
item(442,'P000775','claim','The transmission phase and its inconsistent leading coefficient',r'''The physical scattering convention is \(\psi\sim T e^{-ikx}\) at negative infinity and \(\psi\sim e^{-ikx}+R e^{ikx}\) at positive infinity. With \(T=|T|e^{2i\varphi}\), the notes claim \(\varphi(k)\sim\sum k^{-2n-1}\varphi^{(n)}\) and \(\varphi^{(n)}=(-1)^{n+1}c^{(n)}/[2(2n+1)]\).''',r'''For \(-\psi''+u\psi=k^2\psi\) with real \(u\in C(\mathbb R)\cap L^1(\mathbb R)\) and the written physical incoming-wave convention, the left-Jost Volterra estimate gives \(\log T(k)=-\frac{i}{2k}\int u+O(k^{-2})\). Consequently \(\varphi^{(0)}=-\tfrac14\int u=-c^{(0)}/4\), whereas the notebook gives \(-c^{(0)}/2\). Higher trace identities exist, but this displayed normalization is already false at \(n=0\).''','A',r'''The trace-invariant subject matches \citet{calogerodegasperis1978conservation}; metadata does not verify its agreement with equation (8). The leading-coefficient test is an independent mathematical emendation of the notebook.''',['calogerodegasperis1978conservation'],literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='Only the decisive leading coefficient is repaired; a guessed all-order numerical factor is not substituted for the source.')
item(442,'P000776','claim','Moment evolution, triangularity, and periodicity',r'''For \(\beta(z,t)=\sum\beta_m(t)z^m\), equation (11) says \(\dot C^{(n)}=n\sum_m\beta_m c^{(n+m)}\). Polynomial \(\beta\) makes this triangular; \(\beta_0=i\omega\) allegedly makes all quantities periodic.''',r'''For the pure constant scaling flow \(u_t=\beta_0(xu_x+2u)\), \(u(x,t)=e^{2\beta_0t}u_0(e^{\beta_0t}x)\), and \(\dot c^{(n)}=(2n+1)\beta_0c^{(n)}\). Thus the recorded factor \(n\) is inconsistent even for \(n=0\). Polynomial \(\beta\) gives finite coupling in each row of an infinite upper system, not a closed finite system. Complex periodic scaling does not automatically imply a globally regular periodic real-space solution.''','A',r'''The nonisospectral conservation-law problem belongs to \citet{calogerodegasperis1978conservation}. The all-order nonconstant-beta equation was not verified from full text; the pure-scaling counterexample is conclusive and does not need such verification.''',['calogerodegasperis1978conservation'],literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The capital/lowercase C/c ambiguity is retained, but no alternative moment normalization is defined that would justify the written equation.',confidence='high')
item(442,'P000776','definition','Adjacent Palamodov lecture: caustics and wave fronts',r'''Under a fresh heading dated 21 March 1978, Palamodov illustrates parabolic focusing and the envelope of rays reflected from a spherical mirror, calling the latter a caustic. A surface has horizontal sections equal to successive wave fronts.''',r'''A caustic is the locus of critical values of the projection of a ray congruence; in the planar drawing it is the envelope to which reflected rays are tangent. A time-dependent front may be encoded as a surface in space-time, with its time slices giving front positions. These definitions do not by themselves establish a universal singularity type.''','E',r'''This is expository geometric material in an adjacent Mathematical Society lecture. The supplied fragment contains no lacuna criterion or classification theorem; none is attributed to Calogero.''',speaker='V. P. Palamodov (Mathematical Society, 21 March 1978)',verification='indirect',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',area='Microlocal analysis and singularities')
item(442,'P000776','claim','The illustrated four-thirds front singularity',r'''At the instant of caustic formation the notes underline \(y=x^{4/3}\).''',r'''The plane branch \((x,y)=(s^3,s^4)\) has that real graph and can occur in a special front degeneration. It differs from an ordinary semicubical cusp \((s^2,s^3)\). Which front family, coordinate equivalence, and genericity assumptions Palamodov intended are not recorded.''','F',r'''No exact theorem identifying this drawing and exponent was located. It is preserved as a specific adjacent assertion rather than generalized into a false claim that every caustic has this exponent.''',speaker='V. P. Palamodov',literal='not determinable',verification='unresolved lead',match='none found',confidence='low',area='Microlocal analysis and singularities',status='As of 10 November 2026: the algebraic curve is identifiable, but the unrecorded geometric hypotheses prevent an exact historical theorem match.')
item(442,'P000776','definition','The forward Cauchy fundamental solution',r'''For a hyperbolic operator \(\mathcal L\) of order \(m\), \(\mathcal LE=0\) for \(t>0\), and \(\partial_t^jE|_{t=0}=\delta(x')\delta_{j,m-1}\), \(0\le j<m\). The wave example uses \(\partial_t^2-c^{-2}\Delta\).''',r'''For an operator monic in \(\partial_t^m\), with a noncharacteristic initial hypersurface and hypotheses ensuring the distributional Cauchy problem is well posed, these data define the causal propagation kernel for the last initial datum. Extending it by zero to \(t<0\) gives the corresponding fundamental solution with a point source, subject to the operator normalization.''','E',r'''The page supplies the definition, not a new existence proof. The lacuna theory named in the heading is not actually developed in the surviving material.''',speaker='V. P. Palamodov',verification='indirect',reconstruction='Monicity and Cauchy well-posedness are necessary. The reciprocal c is not corrected: the text does not define c as velocity rather than slowness.',area='Partial differential equations')
correction('P000775','Novikov operator factor order','L=partial^(-1)L3 identified with the earlier vector-field recursion','distinguish gradient recursion partial^(-1)J2 from vector-field recursion J2 partial^(-1)','The scanned inverse derivative is on the left; applying the earlier explicit L to a test function fixes the opposite order for vector fields.')
correction('P000775','transmission-phase formula (8)','phi^(0)=-c^(0)/2 when T=abs(T)exp(2i phi)','phi^(0)=-c^(0)/4 for the displayed Schrodinger normalization','First Born/Riccati term log T=-i integral(u)/(2k); c0=integral(u). Formula requires a normalization repair, not a transcription change.')
correction('P000776','moment equation (11)','d c^(n)/dt=n beta0 c^(n) in the pure scaling case','d c^(n)/dt=(2n+1) beta0 c^(n)','Scan clearly has n. Direct scaling u(x,t)=exp(2beta0 t)u0(exp(beta0 t)x) gives the stated weight; n=0 already contradicts the notebook.')
correction('P000776','lower-page lecture boundary','included within Calogero envelope','V.P.Palamodov at the Mathematical Society,21March1978','Fresh dated Russian heading and speaker visibly occur below the end of the Calogero notes.',kind='provenance')
