talk(443,'1978-03-27','F. Calogero, jointly with A. Degasperis','P000777-P000781','Backlund transformations and polynomial-root dynamics',r'''Wronskian identities connect nonlinear transformations of potentials with multiplicative changes in scattering data. The lecture obtains the KdV recursion by an infinitesimal limit, an elementary soliton transformation, a Riccati equation and nonlinear superposition. A second topic converts a linear PDE for a polynomial into a solvable interacting-root system, with Hermite-zero equilibria. The final linearized equation is missing an identity matrix; its stated matrix spectrum itself is correct.''')
item(443,'P000777','claim','Complete scattering data cannot generally be replaced by reflection alone',r'''After initially including discrete spectrum data, the notes say that eigenvalues are poles of \(R(k)\), with residues \(ic_n^2\), and that \(q\) is determined by \(R\).''',r'''A real decaying potential is determined by admissible complete scattering data: reflection on the real axis together with bound-state eigenvalues and norming constants. Reflection alone is insufficient in this class: the zero potential and nonzero reflectionless solitons all have \(R=0\). Special analyticity or support restrictions can encode more data, but no such class is specified here.''','E',r'''The standard spectral-transform setup is used in \citet{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. The notebook's later assertion that a soliton is added while multiplying R itself provides an internal warning against unconditional uniqueness from R.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction=r'The undeciphered Schrodinger sign is fixed to \(q-k^2\) by the plane-wave asymptotics, an inference rather than a confident reading of the overwritten mark.')
item(443,'P000777-P000778','method','Two-potential Wronskian identities',r'''For two potentials \(q,\widetilde q\), primes denote the second potential, not differentiation. Equations (5)--(6) express \(2ik f(-4k^2)(\widetilde R-R)\) and \((2ik)^2g(-4k^2)(\widetilde R+R)\) as integrals against \(\widetilde\Psi\Psi\).''',r'''Define \(Ih(x)=\int_x^\infty h(s)ds\), \(\Gamma h=(\widetilde q_x+q_x)h+(\widetilde q-q)I((\widetilde q-q)h)\), and \(\Lambda h=h_{xx}-2(\widetilde q+q)h+\Gamma Ih\). With sufficient decay and polynomial f,g, the generalized Wronskian identities pair these operators with products of scattering solutions. Boundary asymptotics supply the reflection differences and sums.''','E',r'''The generalized Wronskian/B\"acklund method is already the subject of \citet[abstract]{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. Full-text coefficient verification of this two-potential normalization was not obtained.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The second Psi(x,t) in (6) must be Psi(x,k) in this stationary identity; the operator notation is made unambiguous.',confidence='medium')
item(443,'P000778-P000779','claim','The adjoint spectral identity for the recursion kernel',r'''\(\int\widetilde\Psi\Psi\,\Lambda F=-4k^2\int\widetilde\Psi\Psi F\); equivalently the pairing with \((\Lambda+4k^2)F\) vanishes.''',r'''For test functions for which integration by parts and the nonlocal integrals have no boundary terms, the product of the two Schr\"odinger solutions is a generalized eigenfunction of the formal adjoint of \(\Lambda\), with eigenvalue \(-4k^2\). This is a pairing identity, not an assertion that \(\Lambda\) is a self-adjoint operator on a Hilbert space.''','E',r'''It is the mechanism behind the spectral substitution in \citet{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}; the identification is partial because the full exact scalar kernel was not read in that publication.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='Test-function domains and the adjoint distinction are supplied.')
item(443,'P000778','construction','A nonlinear Backlund relation induces a rational multiplier',r'''If \(f(\Lambda)(\widetilde q-q)+g(\Lambda)\Gamma1=0\), then \(\widetilde R=\frac{f(-4k^2)-2ikg(-4k^2)}{f(-4k^2)+2ikg(-4k^2)}R\).''',r'''Subject to the preceding Wronskian identities and a nonzero denominator, the displayed multiplier follows by adding their two left sides and solving the resulting scalar linear equation. For a full transformation of potentials, the bound-state and norming data, invertibility, and regularity must also be specified. Arbitrary functions f,g need a justified operator calculus.''','E',r'''General B\"acklund transformations and their spectral meaning predate the seminar in \citet[abstract]{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. The elementary algebraic implication from the recorded identities is exact, even though the paper-level normalization match is partial.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The spectral multiplier is a consequence under hypotheses, not a complete existence theorem for the nonlinear relation.')
item(443,'P000778-P000779','construction','The infinitesimal limit and a sign-inconsistent alternative formula',r'''Let \(\widetilde q=q(x,t+dt)\); then \(\Lambda\to L\), \(\Gamma\to2q_x\), yielding \(q_t=\alpha(L)q_x\), \(R_t=2ik\alpha(-4k^2)R\). Gelfand writes \(L=(\partial^3+2q\partial+2\partial q)\partial^{-1}\).''',r'''At equal potentials, \(L=\partial^2-4q-2q_x\partial^{-1}\), for \(\partial^{-1}h=-Ih\). Thus \(L=(\partial^3-2q\partial-2\partial q)\partial^{-1}\). The written plus signs require replacing the potential by its negative; without that convention change they conflict with (12). Polynomial \(\alpha\) gives the isospectral KdV hierarchy.''','E',r'''The infinitesimal spectral method is pre-existing, \citet{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. The incompatible signs are confirmed from the manuscript and direct operator multiplication.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The sign correction is conditional on keeping the potential convention used throughout this lecture; no source mark is reinterpreted as a minus.')
item(443,'P000779','claim','Products of second-order solutions satisfy a third-order equation',r'''Gelfand remarks that the product of two solutions of a second-order equation satisfies a third-order equation, explaining the recurrence of the KdV operator.''',r'''If \(y_1''=(q-k^2)y_1\) and \(y_2''=(q-k^2)y_2\), then \(z=y_1y_2\) satisfies \(z^{(3)}-4(q-k^2)z'-2q'z=0\). This follows by three differentiations and substitution. It is the symmetric-square equation; both factors must solve the same scalar equation.''','E',r'''The identity is classical and is checked directly here as the algebraic source of the equal-potential adjoint relation. The notebook's list of surnames does not determine a unique additional publication.''',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',verification='indirect',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The explicit differential equation and the same-equation hypothesis are supplied.')
item(443,'P000779','construction','Elementary soliton insertion and Riccati form',r'''For \(g=1,f=2\rho\), \(\widetilde R=-(k+i\rho)(k-i\rho)^{-1}R\). With \(Q=Iq\), \(\widetilde Q_x+Q_x=-\tfrac12(\widetilde Q-Q)(4\rho+\widetilde Q-Q)\); \(Q=0\) gives one soliton.''',r'''The potential relation is a first-order Riccati equation. For \(\rho>0\), the regular branch from the zero seed has \(\widetilde Q=2\rho(\tanh(\rho(x-x_0))-1)\), hence \(\widetilde q=-2\rho^2\operatorname{sech}^2(\rho(x-x_0))\). Its position parameter is norming data absent from the multiplier of R. Time dependence must satisfy the chosen hierarchy equation.''','E',r'''This is the elementary Darboux/B\"acklund mechanism in the earlier spectral-transform framework, \citet{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. The seed solution is directly verified in the notebook's normalization.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The regular branch and norming parameter are essential. Multiplying R=0 cannot by itself distinguish the zero seed from the resulting soliton.')
item(443,'P000779','claim','Generating all integrals by Backlund transformations',r'''The notes say that all integrals are obtained by successive B\"acklund transformation from \(\int u^2dx\).''',r'''B\"acklund transformations can generate relations or generating functions for conserved quantities in specified integrable hierarchies. A literal finite iteration of a transformation of potentials is not, without an additional expansion or functional action, a definition that produces every conservation law from one functional.''','F',r'''\citet{calogerodegasperis1978conservation} is relevant to conserved quantities, but the notebook supplies no parameter expansion, action on functionals, or completeness theorem matching this sentence exactly.''',['calogerodegasperis1978conservation'],literal='not determinable',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='thematic only',confidence='low',status='As of 10 November 2026: the intended generating prescription is not identifiable precisely enough to classify as an open problem or a proved completeness result.')
item(443,'P000779-P000780','theorem','Permutability and nonlinear superposition',r'''Two elementary transformations with parameters \(\rho_1,\rho_2\) commute, since the spectral multipliers commute. Formula (19) gives a rational expression for \(Q_{12}\), with an obscured sign in its denominator.''',r'''For compatible elementary transformations, including their full discrete data, and away from a zero denominator, the four Riccati relations imply \[Q_{12}=Q-\frac{(\rho_1+\rho_2)(Q_1-Q_2)}{\rho_1-\rho_2+\tfrac12(Q_1-Q_2)}.\] Subtracting the two first-level Riccati equations and the two second-level equations eliminates derivatives and gives this formula. This selects a minus between Q1 and Q2 by consistency, not by paleography.''','E',r'''Permutability is a standard B\"acklund property in the method of \citet{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}. The algebraic reconstruction supplies the missing sign; commutativity on reflection coefficients alone would not prove equality of all reconstructed potentials.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The derivative-elimination check is explicit editorial verification of an old relation, not a claimed newly discovered superposition law.')
item(443,'P000780','method','Evolution as a generalized Backlund transformation',r'''Choose time-dependent f,g so that the multiplier equals \(\exp[2ik\alpha(-4k^2)(t-t_0)]\); evolution itself is then a B\"acklund transformation, analogous to a Fourier exponential.''',r'''For a time-independent polynomial \(\alpha\), the continuous scattering data evolve by the displayed exponential. Representing that multiplier as a quotient involving f,g gives a formal generalized B\"acklund relation. In general these f,g are not polynomials, so passing from a spectral identity to the operator equation needs a defined functional calculus and admissible inverse data.''','E',r'''A functional equation relating solutions at two times is explicitly advertised in \citet[abstract]{calogerodegasperis1976inverse}; the specific scalar-operator realization in the notebook was not checked from full text.''',['calogerodegasperis1976inverse'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='For time-dependent alpha the exponent is its time integral. The exact existence theorem is not inferred from a formal quotient alone.')
item(443,'P000780','construction','A polynomial PDE produces interacting-root dynamics',r'''For \(\Psi_{tt}+\tfrac12\Psi_{xx}-x\Psi_x+n\Psi=0\), set \(\Psi=\prod_{j=1}^n(x-x_j(t))\). Then \(\ddot x_j=-x_j+\sum_{k\ne j}(1+2\dot x_j\dot x_k)/(x_j-x_k)\).''',r'''Let \(n\ge0\) and let a monic degree-n polynomial solution have distinct twice-differentiable roots on a time interval. Evaluating the PDE at each root and using logarithmic derivatives gives exactly the displayed ODE. Conversely the root ODE gives the polynomial PDE while roots remain distinct. Collisions require a polynomial or branched continuation, not the singular root coordinates.''','A',r'''The corresponding pole/zero method was submitted 5 July 1977 and published September 1978 in \citet{calogero1978motion}. Publisher metadata and abstract identify the program; the displayed ODE is independently derived from the supplied PDE, not claimed read verbatim in the inaccessible full article.''',['calogero1978motion'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The nonnegative integer restriction, monicity, and simple-root interval are supplied. The marginal names Moser/Makkin/Ero are not used to assign exact priority for this particular ODE.')
item(443,'P000781','construction','Hermite coefficients linearize the root system',r'''\(\Psi=2^{-n}\sum_{m=0}^n c_m(t)H_{n-m}(x)\), \(c_0=1\), gives \(\ddot c_m+mc_m=0\).''',r'''For physicists' Hermite polynomials satisfying \(\tfrac12H_r''-xH_r'+rH_r=0\), substitution diagonalizes the PDE: each coefficient satisfies the indicated oscillator equation. The root/coefficient map is locally invertible at distinct roots, so it solves the nonlinear system by linear evolution followed by finding polynomial roots. The frequencies \(\sqrt m\) are not generally commensurate.''','A',r'''This belongs to the solvable many-body construction of \citet{calogero1978motion}, with the same 1977 submission/1978 publication distinction. The diagonalization follows directly from the displayed Hermite equation.''',['calogero1978motion'],literal='correct',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The Hermite normalization and the local limitation of root coordinates are explicit; no universal periodicity is inferred.')
item(443,'P000781','theorem','Hermite-zero equilibria and Stieltjes relations',r'''At equilibrium \(\bar x_j=\sum_{k\ne j}(\bar x_j-\bar x_k)^{-1}\); these are the Hermite zeros, credited to Stieltjes,1885.''',r'''The monic equilibrium polynomial solves \(\tfrac12\Psi''-x\Psi'+n\Psi=0\), hence equals \(2^{-n}H_n\). At a simple root, \(\Psi''(\bar x_j)/(2\Psi'(\bar x_j))=\sum_{k\ne j}(\bar x_j-\bar x_k)^{-1}\), proving the relations. The distinct real equilibrium is unique up to permutation.''','E',r'''The notebook explicitly treats the root identities as an older Stieltjes result. Their use in the many-body construction is reflected in \citet{calogero1978motion}; the precise1885 bibliographic reference was not independently identified and is not fabricated.''',['calogero1978motion'],verification='indirect',literal='correct',match='partial',reconstruction='The proof and uniqueness follow from the Hermite ODE; the historical year remains a notebook attribution.')
item(443,'P000781','claim','The integer interaction-matrix spectrum and missing identity',r'''For \(A_{jj}=\sum_{k\ne j}(\bar x_j-\bar x_k)^{-2}\), \(A_{jk}=-(\bar x_j-\bar x_k)^{-2}\), the notes write \(\ddot\varepsilon+A\varepsilon=0\), and \(\operatorname{Spec}A=\{0,1,\ldots,n-1\}\).''',r'''Linearization of the displayed nonlinear ODE gives \(\ddot\varepsilon+(I+A)\varepsilon=0\). The Hermite-coefficient linearization has squared frequencies \(1,\ldots,n\), so \(A\) indeed has eigenvalues \(0,\ldots,n-1\), each simple. In particular, the constant vector lies in \(\ker A\), but its center-of-mass motion is an oscillator of frequency1.''','A',r'''The result fits \citet{calogero1978motion}; the missing identity is established directly from the source equations. Even n=1 refutes the uncorrected linearized equation.''',['calogero1978motion'],literal='false as written',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The correct spectrum is retained; only its interpretation as the full dynamical matrix is repaired.')
correction('P000777','equation (6) second scattering factor','Psi(x,t)','Psi(x,k)','Scanned expression uses t, but this is the stationary two-potential identity with common spectral parameter k; mathematical emendation.')
correction('P000777','reflection uniqueness','potential determined by R alone and discrete spectrum by its poles','retain bound-state eigenvalues and norming constants, or specify a restrictive analytic class','Zero and nonzero reflectionless potentials have R identically zero; the notebook first lists discrete information separately.')
correction('P000778','alternative recursion operator','partial cubed plus2q partial plus2partial q','minus signs for both q terms when retaining the earlier potential convention','Scan has plus signs. Multiplication by partial^(-1) otherwise gives plus4q, contrary to the explicitly written minus4q in(12).')
correction('P000780','superposition denominator','Q1 [?] Q2','Q1-Q2, conditional on compatibility with the four displayed Riccati relations','The overwritten scan mark remains unclear; eliminating derivatives algebraically forces the minus sign in this normalization.',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000781','small-oscillation equation','epsilon double dot + A epsilon=0','epsilon double dot +(I+A)epsilon=0','Scan confirms omitted identity; linearization of the -x_j force contributes -epsilon_j, also visible for n=1.')
