talk(444,'1978-04-04','D. B. Fuchs, with I. M. Gelfand','P000782-P000785','Lie cohomology, finite weights, and the Laplacian spectrum',r'''The catalogue date is retained as an identifier; the actual heading on P000782 reads 3 April 1978, while P000785 announces the next meeting on 4 April. The lecture explains Chevalley--Eilenberg homology, the finite-weight Hodge method, Goncharova's theorem, and the new integer spectrum for the positive Witt algebra. The latter appeared later in1978 with Feigin as coauthor. The manuscript confuses eigenvalues with multiplicities and incorrectly identifies distinct eigenvalues with their partition labels; the corrected theorem permits collisions.''')
item(444,'P000782-P000783','definition','The Chevalley-Eilenberg chain and cochain complexes',r'''\(C_q(\mathfrak g)=\Lambda^q\mathfrak g\) and \(d(g_1\wedge\cdots\wedge g_q)=\sum_{s<t}(-1)^{s+t-1}[g_s,g_t]\wedge g_1\wedge\cdots\widehat g_s\cdots\widehat g_t\cdots\wedge g_q\). The dual complex defines cohomology, with continuous functionals in the topological case.''',r'''For a real Lie algebra and trivial coefficients this is the homological Chevalley--Eilenberg differential; the Jacobi identity gives \(d^2=0\). The dual differential raises degree. For formal vector fields one must use continuous cochains in the inverse-limit topology, or equivalently the indicated graded dual, rather than all algebraic functionals on an infinite product.''','E',r'''These standard definitions are recalled in \citet[Section~1]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace}, which explicitly specifies continuous cohomology and trivial coefficients.''',['gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace'],literal='correct',match='exact')
item(444,'P000783-P000784','method','Hodge representatives on finite weight complexes',r'''An inner product identifies chains and cochains, \(\Delta=d\delta+\delta d\), and every homology class has exactly one harmonic representative; \(\Delta a=0\) iff \(da=\delta a=0\).''',r'''For a finite-dimensional chain complex with positive-definite inner products and \(\delta=d^*\), \(\langle\Delta a,a\rangle=\|da\|^2+\|\delta a\|^2\). Orthogonal decomposition gives a unique harmonic representative of each homology class. The argument applies separately to each finite weight block of \(L_1\); an arbitrary infinite-dimensional completion requires closed-range and domain hypotheses.''','E',r'''The finite-block qualification is explicit in \citet[Section~1]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace} and \citet[Proposition~1]{fuchswilmarth2010laplacian}.''',['gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace','fuchswilmarth2010laplacian'],match='exact',reconstruction='The unrestricted opening assertion is read in the finite-weight setting justified later in the lecture, not as a general Hilbert-complex theorem.')
item(444,'P000783-P000784','definition','Formal vector fields and the two vanishing-jet subalgebras',r'''\(W_1=\mathbb R[[x]]\partial_x\), \(L_0=x\mathbb R[[x]]\partial_x\), \(L_1=x^2\mathbb R[[x]]\partial_x\). The formal diffeomorphism group has Lie algebra \(L_0\), and \(W_1/L_0\) has dimension1.''',r'''The group of origin-preserving invertible formal changes of one variable has tangent algebra \(L_0\); its subgroup tangent to the identity has algebra \(L_1\). The constant vector field supplies the one-dimensional quotient of \(W_1\) by \(L_0\) as vector spaces. Formal topology is essential.''','E',r'''These are the algebras in \citet[Section~1]{goncharova1973line} and \citet[Section~1]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace}.''',['goncharova1973line','gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace'],literal='correct',match='exact')
item(444,'P000783-P000784','claim','Finite defect and the unqualified integration obstruction',r'''The notes say that \(W_1\) has no local Lie group and define finite defect as the minimum codimension of a subalgebra integrating to a Lie group; \(L_0\) is the example.''',r'''In the ordinary origin-preserving formal substitution category, translation by a nonzero real constant cannot be substituted into an arbitrary formal series; hence its Lie algebra is \(L_0\), not all \(W_1\). A universal assertion that \(W_1\) integrates to no possible local group requires a specified category, topology, and regularity. The definition of defect depends on that choice.''','F',r'''The distinction among the three formal vector-field algebras is established, but no exact theorem matching the notebook's unrestricted no-local-group statement was identified. Formal group functors over nilpotent bases and ordinary real local Lie groups must not be conflated.''',literal='not determinable',verification='unresolved lead',match='none found',reconstruction='The concrete substitution obstruction is all that the supplied wording supports without a category of integration.',confidence='medium',status='As of 10 November 2026: the restricted substitution statement is clear; the absolute integration assertion remains insufficiently specified.')
item(444,'P000784','claim','Center-free algebras and their derivation algebra',r'''If the center is zero, the Lie algebra of the automorphism group is said to be an over-algebra or subalgebra of the original algebra; the relevant word is unclear.''',r'''The algebraic statement is an injection \(\operatorname{ad}:\mathfrak g\hookrightarrow\operatorname{Der}(\mathfrak g)\) when the center is zero. For a finite-dimensional real Lie algebra, \(\operatorname{Lie}\operatorname{Aut}(\mathfrak g)=\operatorname{Der}(\mathfrak g)\). For arbitrary infinite-dimensional algebras, existence and Lie structure of the automorphism group need further hypotheses.''','E',r'''This is the standard adjoint/derivation construction. The earlier index has 1971-243.4 on automorphisms of formal vector fields, but the current fragment supplies no exact recurrence of a separately indexed problem.''',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='indirect',reconstruction='The algebraic injection determines the direction of containment if over-algebra was intended. The scan itself does not settle the word.')
item(444,'P000784','remark','Previously computed cohomology of W1 and L0',r'''The cohomology of \(W_1\) and \(L_0\) is said to be easily found by Gelfand and Fuchs, in contrast with \(L_1\).''',r'''For continuous real cohomology with trivial coefficients, \(H^*(L_0)\) is the exterior algebra on the one-dimensional linear-part character in degree1. The formal one-variable algebra \(W_1\) has nonzero cohomology in degrees0 and3. The comparison concerns these precise coefficient and topology conventions.''','E',r'''\citet{gelfandfuchs1970formal}, received November1969, is the earlier formal-vector-field computation. Its metadata and abstract were checked; the specific one-dimensional specialization is a reconstructed standard consequence, not a newly claimed full-text verification.''',['gelfandfuchs1970formal'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The note gives no dimensions here; the conventional trivial-coefficient specialization is supplied.')
item(444,'P000784','construction','The positive Witt bracket and finite weight decomposition',r'''\([e_n,e_m]=(m-n)e_{n+m}\), \(e_n=x^{n+1}\partial_x\); the chain differential preserves \(i_1+\cdots+i_q\), so chains split into finite-dimensional complexes \(C_{q,n}\).''',r'''In \(L_1\), \(\deg e_i=i\ge1\). The finite set of strictly increasing positive tuples summing to n spans \(C_{q,n}\); the bracket preserves total weight. The formula after the bracket in notebook (11) must begin with the surviving factor \(e_{i_1}\), not a new fixed \(e_1\).''','E',r'''The same finite-weight construction appears in \citet[Section~1]{goncharova1973line} and \citet[Section~1]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace}.''',['goncharova1973line','gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace'],literal='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The extra subscript is a mathematical repair of a faithful source typo; the weight argument confirms it.')
item(444,'P000784','theorem','Goncharova cohomology and pentagonal weights',r'''For \(q\ge1\), \(\dim H_q(L_1)=2\), with generator weights \((3q^2\pm q)/2\).''',r'''Over characteristic zero, continuous trivial-coefficient \(H^q(L_1)\) is one-dimensional in each of the two weights \((3q^2-q)/2\), \((3q^2+q)/2\), and zero in all other weights; \(H^0\) is one-dimensional in weight0. Weightwise duality gives the corresponding graded homology statement.''','E',r'''\citet[Introduction and main theorem]{goncharova1973line}, submitted21June1972 and published1973, proves the result; its introduction records a preceding short1972 announcement.''',['goncharova1973line'],literal='correct',match='exact')
item(444,'P000784-P000785','definition','The metric matters for the nonzero Laplacian spectrum',r'''The candidates \(e_k\), \(e_k/k!\), and \(\alpha_ke_k\) are considered as orthonormal bases; the lecture chooses \(e_k\), and increasing wedge monomials are orthonormal.''',r'''Fixing \(\langle e_i,e_j\rangle=\delta_{ij}\) determines the induced inner products, adjoint differential, and nonzero spectrum of \(\Delta\). A different rescaling changes the adjoint and generally the spectrum, though harmonic dimensions still give the same cohomology on finite blocks.''','A',r'''This exact metric is specified by \citet[Section~1]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace} and in \citet[before Theorem~2]{fuchswilmarth2010laplacian}.''',['gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace','fuchswilmarth2010laplacian'],literal='correct',match='exact')
item(444,'P000785','theorem','Integer Laplacian eigenvalues and their corrected multiplicities',r'''For gap-three partitions \(I=(i_1<\cdots<i_q)\), the notes display \(E(I)=\sum_s\binom{i_s}{3}-\sum_{s<t}i_si_t\), but call it a multiplicity and claim distinct eigenvalues are counted by such partitions.''',r'''For the fixed orthonormal metric, the total weight-n chain space has eigenvalues E(I), for gap-three partitions of n. Define \(\alpha(I)\) to count the first part if \(i_1\ge3\), plus each gap strictly greater than3. A label contributes multiplicity \(2^{\alpha(I)}\); if labels yield the same E, add these multiplicities. Thus E is the eigenvalue, not its multiplicity. At weight25, \(I=(1,10,14)\) and \(J=(3,7,15)\) both give320, with total multiplicity \(4+8=12\).''','A',r'''\citet{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace} was received14June1978. \citet[Theorem~2]{fuchswilmarth2010laplacian} states the corrected version and credits an important1993 correction by Weinstein. The chapter independently checks integer CE matrices through weight22 and the weight25 collision; this verifies examples, not a new general proof.''',['gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace','fuchswilmarth2010laplacian'],literal='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The corrected multiplicity rule comes from the later source. The explicit collision is an independent finite verification and is labelled as such in the development appendix.',status='As of10November2026: the corrected spectral theorem is established; the notebook is not an open problem. Its distinct-eigenvalue and multiplicity wording requires the stated repairs.')
item(444,'P000785','problem','Nonnegativity and the two zero-eigenvalue partitions',r'''Exercise: E(I) is nonnegative and vanishes exactly for \((1,4,7,\ldots,3q-2)\) and \((2,5,8,\ldots,3q-1)\).''',r'''For every nonempty increasing positive tuple with consecutive gaps at least3, \(E(I)\ge0\); equality holds exactly for the two displayed arithmetic progressions. Their weights are the two pentagonal numbers in Goncharova's theorem.''','A',r'''The assertion is explicitly given with \citet[Theorem~1]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace} and reiterated immediately before \citet[Theorem~2]{fuchswilmarth2010laplacian}. It is a resolved combinatorial exercise, not an undeveloped research problem.''',['gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace','fuchswilmarth2010laplacian'],literal='correct',match='exact')
item(444,'P000785','construction','Vandermonde-cubed harmonic representatives',r'''Identify \(e_i\) with \(z^i\). Multiply \(\prod_{i<j}(z_i-z_j)^3\) by \(\prod_i z_i\) or \(\prod_i z_i^2\), expand the alternating polynomial, and replace alternants by wedge monomials.''',r'''Under the alternating-polynomial realization of \(\Lambda^qL_1\), the two displayed polynomials determine nonzero harmonic chains in weights \((3q^2-q)/2\) and \((3q^2+q)/2\), respectively. They represent the two homology classes in degreeq, up to overall nonzero normalization and the sign convention for alternants.''','A',r'''Explicit harmonic representatives are supplied by \citet[Section~2]{gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace}. The chapter also verifies both boundary and adjoint-boundary vanish for q=1,2,3,4 with exact arithmetic, as a finite check of the historical result.''',['gelfandfeiginfuchs1978laplace'],literal='correct',match='exact',reconstruction='The notebook says eigenvectors; in these two cases the eigenvalue is zero. Overall alternant normalization does not affect the represented line.')
