talk(445,'','M. D. Kruskal (adjacent lecture)','P000786-P000787','Catalogue heading 1978 and a lecture on surreal numbers',r'''Although the catalogue entry is only a year heading, both supplied pages contain substantial mathematics from Kruskal's lecture. The scan reads 30 October 1978, with November a paleographic alternative; it is adjacent later material, not a lecture of the principal 1977--1978 academic-year sequence. The pages develop sign sequences, simplicity, cuts, and addition of surreal numbers, and mention Conway's book and Knuth's narrative exposition. No research problem is posed.''',kind='other')
item(445,'P000786','definition','Ordinal sign sequences and their numerical order',r'''A number is a map from a well-ordered set to two arrows. Compare arrows lexicographically; the empty path is zero and an infinite upward sequence is \(\omega\). A binary tree and periodic-arrow symbols are drawn.''',r'''A surreal sign sequence is a function \(s:\alpha\to\{-,+\}\) for a set ordinal \(\alpha\). At the first differing place, compare \(-<\text{termination}<+\). This convention orders unequal lengths as well as equal lengths. The empty sequence is zero; \(+\), \(++\), and \(+-\) represent \(1,2,1/2\), while the all-plus sequence of length \(\omega\) is \(\omega\). A periodic pattern needs its ordinal length specified.''','E',r'''This is an alternative realization of the numbers constructed by cuts in \citet[Chapter~0, pp.~4--7]{conway1976numbers}. The original book appeared in1976; the opened text is its2001 second-edition reprint. The specific sign-sequence convention is a reconstruction of Kruskal's notes, not claimed quoted from those pages.''',['conway1976numbers'],reconstruction='Well-ordered domains are replaced by their ordinal order types and the missing termination comparison is supplied.',match='partial',verification='indirect')
item(445,'P000786-P000787','definition','Simplicity, truncations, and canonical cuts',r'''An earlier number is on the path from0 to the given number. Truncations split into earlier smaller and earlier greater numbers, and \(x=\{x\rfloor:x\rceil\}\).''',r'''The simplicity relation means proper initial segment of the sign sequence, not merely a smaller numerical value. Let \(L_x\) and \(R_x\) consist of proper truncations respectively less and greater than x. Then x is the unique simplest number strictly between these two sets, written \(x=\{L_x\mid R_x\}\).''','E',r'''The cut construction and distinction between a number and its many forms are explained in \citet[Chapter~0, pp.~4--6]{conway1976numbers}.''',['conway1976numbers'],literal='correct',match='partial',reconstruction='Canonical initial-segment simplicity is distinguished from the broader birthday ordering; different numbers can be born at the same stage.')
item(445,'P000787','theorem','The simplest number between two separated sets',r'''If A,B are sets of numbers and every a in A is less than every b in B, there exists a unique earliest x with \(a<x<b\); proof by transfinite induction.''',r'''For sets \(A,B\) of surreal numbers with \(A<B\), there is a unique surreal of minimum birthday in the interval \(\{x:A<x<B\}\); equivalently it is the simplest element of that interval. Either set may be empty. Set-sizedness is essential: no surreal exceeds every member of the proper class of ordinals.''','E',r'''The construction rule of \citet[Chapter~0, p.~4]{conway1976numbers} is exactly the separated-cut operation. The notebook's formulation uses the equivalent sign-tree simplicity theorem.''',['conway1976numbers'],literal='correct',match='partial',reconstruction='The quantifier is over sets, as the manuscript says; it is not extended to arbitrary proper classes.')
item(445,'P000787','construction','Recursive addition and ordered additive-group laws',r'''Define \(x+y=\{x\rfloor+y,x+y\rfloor:x\rceil+y,x+y\rceil\}\). The notes prove \(x+0=x\), assert monotonicity, commutativity and associativity, and obtain the opposite by reversing arrows.''',r'''Well-founded recursion on both arguments defines \[x+y=\{x^L+y,x+y^L\mid x^R+y,x+y^R\}.\] It makes the surreal numbers an ordered abelian group: addition is strictly increasing in either argument, zero is the identity, and the negative reverses all signs. Associativity has the usual form \((x+y)+z=x+(y+z)\). The phrase simplest monotone number abbreviates these recursive inequalities; monotonicity alone is not a uniqueness axiom for addition.''','E',r'''The addition and negation formulas are explicitly given in \citet[Chapter~0, p.~5]{conway1976numbers}; the elementary zero calculation occupies pp.~6--7. No new arithmetic or unresolved associativity question is present.''',['conway1976numbers'],match='exact',reconstruction='The ambiguous sign in the handwritten associativity expression is interpreted contextually as plus, not declared visibly resolved.')
correction('P000786-P000787','catalogue heading versus embedded lecture','1978 heading without mathematics','adjacent Kruskal lecture, probably30October1978, possible30November','The scan includes named speaker, date and two pages of surreal-number mathematics. Month uncertainty retained.',kind='provenance')
correction('P000787','associativity sign','x+(y [?] z)','x+(y+z) as the intended additive-group law','The sign is not paleographically certain; surrounding addition properties determine the mathematical interpretation.',kind='ambiguity')
