talk(451,'','Unidentified preceding lecturer; I. M. Gelfand and A. A. Belavin','P000810-P000812','Catalogue heading 1979 with representation cohomology and instanton questions',r'''The catalogue year heading encloses two different pieces of adjacent1979 material. P000810--P000811 continue an unidentified preceding lecture on extensions, continuous cohomology, smooth vectors, Harish--Chandra modules, and semidirect products. P000812 is explicitly dated12February1979 and gives a physics reading programme followed by Belavin's ADHM matrix-parameter question and a short gauge/confinement fragment. The reading list is provenance evidence, not itself a collection of mathematical theorems. Its path-integral coauthor is written Gibbs, plausibly a notebook error for Hibbs.''',kind='other')
item(451,'P000810','definition','Infinitesimal characters and algebraic block separation',r'''If central elements act on E by scalars, \(u e=\chi(u)e\), then \(\chi\in\operatorname{Hom}(Z(\mathfrak g),\mathbb C)\) is an infinitesimal character. The following page refers to a lemma about distinct infinitesimal characters.''',r'''Here \(Z(\mathfrak g)\) means \(Z(U(\mathfrak g))\), and \(\chi\) is a unital algebra homomorphism. If E,F are algebraic \(U(\mathfrak g)\)-modules with distinct scalar central characters, every extension of E by F splits: a central element with distinct scalar values yields a polynomial spectral projector onto F. For topological representation spaces its derived action may be unbounded, so this argument does not automatically produce a continuous splitting.''','E',r'''The algebraic assertion is the standard separation of central-character blocks. The paper \citet[Section~V(C)]{delorme1977cohomologie} supplies the contemporaneous representation-cohomology context; the precise unnamed lemma is reconstructed algebraically, not identified as a numbered theorem of that paper.''',['delorme1977cohomologie'],verification='indirect',match='partial',reconstruction='The notes begin with scalar action as an assumption. An unrestricted Schur-lemma assertion for arbitrary topological irreducibles is not added.')
item(451,'P000810','construction','Relative first cohomology classifies split-on-h extensions',r'''Relative cocycles satisfy \(f|_{\mathfrak h}=0\) and describe extensions trivial over \(\mathfrak h\). The relation between relative and absolute \(H^1\) is written with a membership sign. The cocycle differential is displayed.''',r'''For algebraic modules E,F, set \(M=\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathbb C}(E,F)\), with \((xT)e=x(Te)-T(xe)\). Relative1-cocycles are absolute cocycles vanishing on \(\mathfrak h\), automatically \(\mathfrak h\)-equivariant; divide by coboundaries from \(M^{\mathfrak h}\). Their classes classify extensions equipped with an \(\mathfrak h\)-splitting up to equivalence. The forgetful map \(H^1(\mathfrak g,\mathfrak h;M)\hookrightarrow H^1(\mathfrak g;M)\) is injective, with image those extensions that split over \(\mathfrak h\).''','E',r'''This is the classical degree-one cocycle/extension interpretation. Its injectivity is immediate: if a relative cocycle is \(dm\), its vanishing on \(\mathfrak h\) forces \(m\in M^{\mathfrak h}\).''',verification='indirect',match='partial',literal='false as written',reconstruction='The membership symbol is replaced by the canonical injection. The notes use the negative of one common differential convention; its cocycle equation is equivalent.')
item(451,'P000810','construction','Continuous cocycles and topologically split extensions',r'''A continuous G-module is a topological vector space with continuous action. A cocycle \(f:G\to\operatorname{Hom}(E,F)\) is required to act jointly continuously in g and e; an alternative uses smooth vectors.''',r'''For continuous representations E,F, a cocycle with jointly continuous evaluation defines the triangular action on \(F\oplus E\). Modulo continuous changes of splitting, it classifies extensions that admit a continuous linear splitting. A general exact sequence of topological vector spaces need not split as vector spaces continuously, so these cocycles need not classify every topological extension.''','E',r'''The continuity and cochain conventions are set out for Hilbert modules in \citet[Section~0]{delorme1977cohomologie} and for Frechet modules in \citet[Sections~2--4]{blanc1979cohomologie}. The extension category is an explicit reconstruction.''',['delorme1977cohomologie','blanc1979cohomologie'],match='partial',reconstruction='The required continuous splitting and a topology on the Hom space cannot be suppressed.')
item(451,'P000810','theorem','Smooth-vector regularization and the van Est comparison',r'''For E equal to the trivial one-dimensional module, \(H^1(G,F)\cong H^1(G,F_\infty)\cong H^1(\mathfrak g,\mathfrak k;F_\infty)\), attributed respectively to Delorme--Blanc and van Est.''',r'''For a finite-dimensional connected Lie group G and a continuous Frechet G-module F, smoothing compares continuous group cohomology with differentiable cohomology of the Frechet smooth-vector module \(F^\infty\). Under the usual van Est hypotheses, including maximal compact K and the contractibility of \(G/K\), the latter agrees with relative Lie algebra cohomology; the K-invariance, not merely \(\mathfrak k\)-invariance, must be imposed if K is disconnected.''','B',r'''The smooth-vector assertion is read in \citet[Theorem~5.2, pp.~152--153]{blanc1979cohomologie}, published1979; an exact first-circulation date was not established. The second arrow is a classical van Est comparison; this audit verifies it only indirectly through the sources' references and framework.''',['blanc1979cohomologie'],match='partial',reconstruction='The module class, continuous cohomology and compact-subgroup invariance are essential. The date of the preceding unidentified lecture is not fixed by P812.')
item(451,'P000810','example','A real unipotent extension and the compact-period obstruction',r'''For \(\mathbb R\), \(t\mapsto\begin{pmatrix}1&at\\0&1\end{pmatrix}\) is a nontrivial extension of the trivial module. The following sentence denies a general group/algebra correspondence and says to use cohomology trivial on the compact subgroup.''',r'''For \(a\ne0\) this is a nonsplit representation of the additive real group and of its one-dimensional Lie algebra. It does not descend to \(\mathbb R/T\mathbb Z\) for \(T\ne0\), because its value at T is not the identity. Thus it illustrates a period obstruction for a quotient group, not failure to integrate this Lie-algebra module to the simply connected group \(\mathbb R\).''','E',r'''The computation is elementary and consistent with the role of relative cohomology in the preceding comparison. The notebook does not name the compact quotient, so that part is explicitly an explanatory reconstruction.''',verification='indirect',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The ambiguous denial of correspondence is not read as a claim that the displayed real-group representation fails to exist.')
item(451,'P000811','claim','Non-Hausdorff first cohomology of the affine group',r'''For an irreducible unitary representation of the line's affine group, \(B^1\) is dense in \(Z^1\) but \(\dim Z^1/B^1=\infty\); weak containment of the trivial representation is cited as the reason.''',r'''One must distinguish ordinary continuous \(H^1=Z^1/B^1\) from reduced \(\overline H^1=Z^1/\overline{B^1}\). For the noncharacter irreducible unitary representations of the connected affine group, the latter vanishes, while the former can be nonzero and non-Hausdorff. The manuscript does not specify its chosen irreducible realization or a proof of the stated infinite algebraic dimension.''','E',r'''The density assertion is covered by \citet[Theorem~V.6]{delorme1977cohomologie}; its introduction p.~284 credits an earlier affine-group calculation to B. E. Johnson. The infinite-dimensional quotient claim was not independently matched to an exact full-text statement and remains a limitation of this identification.''',['delorme1977cohomologie'],match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The closure is decisive. Weak containment alone is not substituted for every needed hypothesis in a general dimension theorem.')
item(451,'P000811','claim','Semisimple cohomology and a contradictory orthogonal-group fragment',r'''The notes say the nonzero unitary H1 cases for semisimple groups are known, then write under nonsemisimple groups that \(SO_0(m,n)\) has no nonunitary representations with nonzero cohomology.''',r'''For a connected real simple Lie group whose Lie algebra is neither \(\mathfrak{so}(n,1)\) nor \(\mathfrak{su}(n,1)\), every irreducible unitary representation has trivial first continuous cohomology. This valid theorem does not repair the literal notebook sentence: most \(SO_0(m,n)\) are semisimple, and nonunitary is not the theorem's unitary hypothesis.''','F',r'''The precise unitary theorem is \citet[Theorem~V.4]{delorme1977cohomologie}. The manuscript's two conflicting words are confirmed in the scan; several different corrections are possible, so none is silently adopted.''',['delorme1977cohomologie'],literal='false as written',match='partial',confidence='low',status='As of10November2026: the nearby unitary theorem is established; the exact intended orthogonal-group assertion is not identifiable.')
item(451,'P000811','claim','An unspecified SL2 topological-extension counterexample',r'''The infinitesimal-character lemma is false in the first continuous-Hom approach; a counterexample for \(SL(2,\mathbb R)\) is mentioned. It becomes true for smooth modules, whose cohomology is harder to calculate.''',r'''An algebraic central spectral projector need not define a continuous operator on a Hilbert globalization, while the center acts on smooth vectors. Therefore the category of extensions matters. Without the two representations, their topology, the central characters and the extension map, the alleged counterexample cannot be reconstructed uniquely.''','F',r'''The search through \citet{delorme1977cohomologie} and \citet{blanc1979cohomologie} established the surrounding comparison theory but did not identify this exact counterexample. No new open problem is inferred from a missing example.''',['delorme1977cohomologie','blanc1979cohomologie'],verification='unresolved lead',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',confidence='low')
item(451,'P000811','method','K-finite vectors and algebraic extension computations',r'''Replace representations by their K-finite vectors. For all irreducible Harish--Chandra modules of SL2 one can compute \(H^1(\mathfrak g,\mathfrak k;\operatorname{Hom}(E_K,F_K))\), without topology.''',r'''In the admissible \((\mathfrak g,K)\)-module category, finite-dimensional K-types make K-splittings available. Relative cocycles describe first extensions, with K-equivariance and the appropriate K-finite part of Hom. For SL2 the resulting calculation depends on the two modules and their infinitesimal characters; no dimensions for individual pairs are supplied here.''','E',r'''The transition to K-finite vectors is used concretely in \citet[Section~V(C), especially p.~319]{delorme1977cohomologie}. The blanket all-pairs computability sentence is an announced method, not a supplied classification table verified by this audit.''',['delorme1977cohomologie'],match='partial',reconstruction='The category and K-invariance replace an unrestricted algebraic Hom formula.')
item(451,'P000811','construction','Semidirect products and dual orbits',r'''For \(G=B\rtimes A\), \(B=\mathbb R^n\), the A-action determines the product; consider an orbit X in \(B^*\), with the affine group as example.''',r'''Given \(\rho:A\to GL(B)\), multiplication is \((b,a)(b',a')=(b+\rho(a)b',aa')\). The contragredient action on \(B^*\) acts on the characters \(b\mapsto e^{i\langle b,\xi\rangle}\). Orbit and stabilizer data are inputs to induction; the fragment does not state the regularity conditions or a classification theorem for all semidirect products.''','E',r'''Semidirect products are part of the older representation-theoretic framework discussed in \citet[Introduction and Section~V(D)]{delorme1977cohomologie}. Only the displayed setup is attributed to this fragment.''',['delorme1977cohomologie'],match='thematic only',reconstruction='No unrecorded full Mackey theorem is counted as an additional notebook assertion.')
item(451,'P000812','construction','The quaternionic ADHM equations and their quotient',r'''Belavin asks about quaternion matrices A,B of size \(N\times(N+1)\), with \(M=xA+B\) and \(MM^+\) real for every quaternion x, modulo \(M\mapsto GMV\), \(VV^+=1\).''',r'''In a consistent transposed quaternionic ADHM convention, impose the reality equations and the nondegeneracy/rank conditions needed to obtain a smooth charge-N SU2 instanton on the conformal four-sphere. The equivalence group is \(GL(N,\mathbb R)\) on the rank-N factor and \(Sp(N+1)\) on the quaternionic ambient factor. The order of quaternion multiplication must be fixed when transposing the usual \((N+1)\times N\) convention.''','E',r'''The exact linear-algebra classification and these groups occur in \citet[pp.~185--186]{atiyahhitchindrinfeldmanin1978instantons}, received16December1977 and published6March1978, before the actual12February1979 notebook meeting. The printed source also proves completeness, not merely necessity of the equations.''',['atiyahhitchindrinfeldmanin1978instantons'],match='exact',reconstruction='The type of G, nondegeneracy, reality structure and transpose convention are missing from the notebook. Dropping them admits singular or spurious pairs.',speaker='A. A. Belavin',area='Mathematical physics')
item(451,'P000812','question','A beautiful parametrization of instanton matrices',r'''There should be \(8N-3\) parameters in all; find a beautiful parametrization of such matrix pairs. The question is humorously attributed to a Chernogolovka competition.''',r'''For \(N\ge1\), the unframed smooth SU2 instanton moduli space on \(S^4\) has real dimension \(8N-3\). The ADHM quotient is an algebraic description; obtaining explicit coordinates, solving the quadratic constraints, or determining global topology are different tasks. Beautiful supplies no criterion distinguishing them, so the residual question has no single precise solved/open status.''','F',r'''The parameter count and full construction are on p.~186 of \citet{atiyahhitchindrinfeldmanin1978instantons}; p.~187 expressly distinguishes construction from extracting moduli geometry and topology. This does not establish an exact later solution to the notebook's unspecified aesthetic request.''',['atiyahhitchindrinfeldmanin1978instantons'],match='partial',literal='not determinable',reconstruction='The established count is separated from the undefined requested parametrization; a research-problem ID is not manufactured.',speaker='A. A. Belavin',area='Mathematical physics',confidence='medium')
item(451,'P000812','remark','Local gauge transformations and an unfinished confinement argument',r'''A heading promises exactly solvable two-dimensional field-theory questions and a naive Wilson argument about non-escape. It writes \(\delta S=0\) and \(\psi(x)\mapsto e^{i\alpha(x)}\psi(x)\).''',r'''Stationarity of an action gives its Euler--Lagrange equations. In a gauge theory a local phase transformation of matter is accompanied by a transformation of the connection so that covariant derivatives transform covariantly. These two lines alone imply neither confinement nor a Wilson-loop area law; no action, gauge dynamics or limiting regime is recorded.''','F',r'''The fragment is thematically related to \citet{wilson1974confinement}, but supplies no proposition matching that paper's lattice strong-coupling confinement argument. Earlier GP-1975-10 concerns a definite confinement problem; this fragment is only a thematic link and does not justify reusing its problem ID.''',['wilson1974confinement'],verification='publisher metadata checked',match='thematic only',literal='not determinable',area='Mathematical physics',confidence='low')
correction('P000810','relative/absolute cohomology sign','H1(relative) is a member of H1(absolute)','canonical injection in degree1, with image the extensions splitting over h','Scan confirms membership sign. A relative coboundary is an absolute coboundary from an h-invariant element.')
correction('P000811','orthogonal-group cohomology sentence','nonsemisimple SO0(m,n), no nonunitary representations with nonzero cohomology','intended statement unresolved; do not silently replace both negations','Scan confirms both words; Delorme1977 ThmV.4 concerns unitary representations of simple groups excluding so(n,1),su(n,1).',kind='ambiguity')
correction('P000812','catalogue heading and embedded date','YEAR1979 without a dated lecture','12February1979 for the Gelfand/Belavin page; earlier two pages undated continuations','The heading12/II-79 is plainly visible; the year heading is retained as catalogue envelope.',kind='provenance')
