talk(457,'1978-03-05','D. B. Fuchs; adjacent A. A. Beilinson and V. I. Ogievetsky','P000823-P000831','Graded Lie algebras with adjacent ambitwistor and supersymmetry notes',r'''The catalogue date1978-03-05 is retained only as the envelope identifier. P000824 actually reads5March1979. P000823 belongs to a preceding Beilinson intervention on the flag/ambitwistor transform; P000824--P000829 are Fuchs on Cartan prolongations and Kac--Moody constructions; P000830--P000831 begin Ogievetsky's19March1979 lecture, headed Supergravity but recording only the super-Poincare algebra and superspace kinematics. The three speakers and dates must not be collapsed into a Fuchs lecture of1978. The principal mathematical omissions are simplicity/irreducibility in classification, complexification in the rank-one test, transposed indices in the Serre relation, and the central quotient in loop realizations.''')
item(457,'P000823','construction','The complete-flag double fibration',r'''Beilinson considers full flags in four-space, projecting to \(G(4,2)\) by forgetting the line and hyperplane, and to \(Q\subset\mathbb P^3\times\mathbb P^{3*}\) by forgetting the plane.''',r'''Let \(F=\{\ell\subset P\subset H\subset\mathbb C^4:\dim(\ell,P,H)=(1,2,3)\}\), \(p:F\to\operatorname{Gr}(2,4)\), and \(q:F\to Q=\{(\ell,H):\ell\subset H\}\). A q-fiber is \(\mathbb P(H/\ell)\cong\mathbb P^1\); a p-fiber is \(\mathbb P(P)\times\mathbb P((\mathbb C^4/P)^*)\cong\mathbb P^1\times\mathbb P^1\). These are the compactified null-ray and null-direction correspondences.''','E',r'''The corresponding local ambitwistor construction is reviewed in \citet[Section~2]{masonskinner2006ambitwistor}, with explicit attribution to1978 work of Witten and Isenberg--Yasskin--Green. The flag-space description itself is directly recoverable from the supplied diagram.''',['masonskinner2006ambitwistor'],speaker='A. A. Beilinson',area='Algebraic geometry and mathematical physics',literal='correct',match='partial',reconstruction='The subspace dimensions and both fibers are supplied explicitly; these are complex projective spaces.')
item(457,'P000823','construction','Parallel sections along null curves and descent',r'''A bundle with connection on the Grassmannian is canonically trivial along each CP1 because curvature vanishes in dimension1 and the fundamental group is zero. Pull back to F, descend to Q, and obtain a holomorphic bundle trivial on CP1 times CP1.''',r'''For a holomorphic connection, curvature is a holomorphic2-form and its restriction to a complex curve vanishes. On a simply connected curve, parallel sections identify the bundle with its space of horizontal sections tensored with its structure sheaf; there is no distinguished basis of that space. On an admissible complex open region, the spaces of sections along null curves form the descended bundle, provided the fibers are connected and simply connected and the analytic descent hypotheses hold. A smooth real connection does not have zero curvature merely because the curve has complex dimension1.''','E',r'''This is the parallel-section construction of \citet[Section~2, Theorem~1 and footnote~1]{masonskinner2006ambitwistor}. The2005 preprint/2006 publication restates the1978 correspondence; it is not evidence of a new1979 theorem by the adjacent speaker.''',['masonskinner2006ambitwistor'],speaker='A. A. Beilinson',area='Algebraic geometry and mathematical physics',match='partial',reconstruction='Holomorphicity, the local domain and the meaning of canonical are essential additions. The notebook does not specify a nontrivial global holomorphic connection on the entire compact Grassmannian.')
item(457,'P000823','claim','The reverse ambitwistor correspondence and missing field equations',r'''The final arrow is \((E,\nabla)\longleftrightarrow\widetilde E\), with \(\widetilde E\) trivial on each CP1 times CP1; no inverse construction or field equation is written.''',r'''In a suitable local complex domain, a holomorphic ambitwistor bundle trivial on the quadrics of null directions reconstructs a space-time connection by evaluation and parallel transport. Triviality on these quadrics is not by itself the Yang--Mills equation. In the classical extension formulation the latter is a condition that the bundle extend to the third formal neighbourhood of the ambitwistor quadric.''','E',r'''The inverse construction and the distinct field-equation condition are separated in \citet[Theorems~1--2]{masonskinner2006ambitwistor}. The notebook records only the former schematic correspondence; the latter is explanatory context, not an additional notebook theorem.''',['masonskinner2006ambitwistor'],speaker='A. A. Beilinson',area='Algebraic geometry and mathematical physics',match='partial',reconstruction='Local analytic hypotheses are supplied. No global equivalence or Yang--Mills claim is inferred from the two-headed arrow alone.')
item(457,'P000824','definition','Grading and transitivity in the adjoint action',r'''A graded Lie algebra has \([\mathfrak g_i,\mathfrak g_j]\subset\mathfrak g_{i+j}\). The bracket gives maps to \(\operatorname{Hom}(\mathfrak g_{-1},\mathfrak g_{i-1})\) and \(\operatorname{Hom}(\mathfrak g_1,\mathfrak g_{i+1})\), assumed injective in opposite degree ranges.''',r'''The degree-zero part is a Lie algebra and each homogeneous part is its module. The stated injectivity means that a nonnegative-degree element centralizing \(\mathfrak g_{-1}\), or a nonpositive-degree element centralizing \(\mathfrak g_1\), is zero. For the classification discussed later one also imposes finite-dimensional homogeneous pieces, appropriate generation and irreducibility, and a characteristic-zero algebraically closed ground field.''','E',r'''The graded, irreducible and graded-simple conventions are given in \citet[Introduction and Chapter~I]{kac1968graded}, submitted13February1968. Its simple explicitly means absence of homogeneous ideals, which matters below.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='correct',match='exact',reconstruction='The additional classification assumptions are separated from the elementary definition.')
item(457,'P000824','claim','Four Cartan families and the missing classification hypotheses',r'''Bounded-below grading and the stated transitivity are followed by a claim that there are four series: all polynomial vector fields, divergence-free, Hamiltonian, and contact fields.''',r'''The four simple Cartan-type families over \(\mathbb C\) are W, S, H and K, interpreted in the appropriate polynomial graded or completed formal category. The broader transitive irreducible classification also includes conformal extensions before imposing simplicity. Boundedness and transitivity alone do not imply the four-family conclusion. The contact grading has depth2 when the contact coordinate has weight2 and the other coordinates weight1.''','E',r'''Compare \citet[Chapter~III, Section~1; Theorems~4--5]{kac1968graded}. The four simple types in Theorem4 and the six Cartan types in Theorem5 explain why simplicity cannot be omitted.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='Infinite dimension, irreducibility, simplicity and completion conventions are restored rather than attributed to words absent from the notebook.')
item(457,'P000824-P000825','construction','The maximal Cartan prolongation and Jacobi symmetry',r'''For depth1, the notes say the whole algebra is recovered from \(\mathfrak g_{-1},\mathfrak g_0\), and define successive symmetric maps by the Jacobi identity.''',r'''Let V be finite-dimensional and \(\mathfrak h\subset\mathfrak{gl}(V)\). Its maximal prolongation has \(\mathfrak g_{-1}=V\), \(\mathfrak g_0=\mathfrak h\), and \[\mathfrak g_k=(S^{k+1}V^*\otimes V)\cap(S^kV^*\otimes\mathfrak h),\quad k\ge1.\] The symmetry of \(f(v_1,\ldots,v_k)v_{k+1}\) follows from the Jacobi identity and \([V,V]=0\). Every transitive algebra with these lower pieces embeds in the prolongation; equality is a maximality condition, not automatic.''','E',r'''The construction is spelled out in \citet[Section~2.2]{ottazziwarhurst2011contact}, which treats it as the classical Singer--Sternberg prolongation; its earlier history includes \citet{guilleminquillensternberg1967infinite}.''',['ottazziwarhurst2011contact','guilleminquillensternberg1967infinite'],literal='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The notebook changes an inclusion into an equality when choosing the maximal prolongation; that choice is made explicit.')
item(457,'P000825','construction','The inductively defined bracket on a prolongation',r'''To define \([\alpha,\beta]\), specify its bracket with every \(\delta\in\mathfrak g_{-1}\) and use Jacobi to reduce to previously defined degrees. Simplicity is an extra condition; most examples are finite-dimensional.''',r'''For homogeneous prolongation elements, define contraction of the bracket by \([[\alpha,\beta],v]=[\alpha,[\beta,v]]-[\beta,[\alpha,v]]\). Transitivity gives uniqueness, and the symmetry conditions yield existence and Jacobi. Equivalently use the commutator of their polynomial-vector-field realizations. The phrase most finite-dimensional is heuristic, not a measure-theoretic genericity theorem.''','E',r'''The recursive bracket and realization are part of the prolongation framework presented in \citet[Section~2]{ottazziwarhurst2011contact}.''',['ottazziwarhurst2011contact'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='The finite-dimensional faithful input and the equivalent polynomial realization justify the inductive recipe.')
item(457,'P000826','construction','The polynomial vector-field realization',r'''Take polynomial fields on \(\mathfrak g_{-1}\) whose linear Taylor part at every point belongs to \(\mathfrak g_0\); this is the algebra constructed by prolongation.''',r'''For \(\mathfrak h\subset\mathfrak{gl}(V)\), the polynomial fields X with \(DX(v)\in\mathfrak h\) for all v form the maximal prolongation. Comparing homogeneous coefficients gives the symmetric-tensor intersection in the preceding item. Closure under brackets follows by differentiating \([X,Y]=DY\,X-DX\,Y\) and using that derivatives of h-valued polynomials remain h-valued and h is closed under commutators.''','E',r'''This is the flat-structure interpretation explained before \citet[Theorem~2.3]{ottazziwarhurst2011contact}; the notebook gives the same coefficient criterion.''',['ottazziwarhurst2011contact'],literal='correct',match='exact',reconstruction='The Jacobian convention is fixed; the coefficient proof is an explanatory verification of the old construction.')
item(457,'P000826','theorem','The complex rank-one criterion for infinite type',r'''Wilson's lemma: the Cartan prolongation is infinite-dimensional iff some \(\xi\in\mathfrak g_0\) has rank1; the speaker refers to Guillemin and two coauthors. Reductivity and irreducibility are separately mentioned.''',r'''For finite-dimensional complex V and \(\mathfrak h\subset\mathfrak{gl}(V)\), its prolongation has nonzero terms in arbitrarily high degree iff h contains a nonzero rank-one endomorphism. For real V apply this test to the complexification. No irreducibility or reductivity is required for this finite-type criterion; such conditions belong to classification refinements. A rank-one element \(v\otimes\xi\) directly produces \(v\otimes\xi^{k+1}\) in every prolongation degree.''','E',r'''The theorem and proof are read in \citet[Theorem~2.3, Sections~2.3--2.4]{ottazziwarhurst2011contact}, which identifies \citet{guilleminquillensternberg1967infinite} as the first published proof. The notebook's Wilson attribution may concern a later proof; it was not independently resolved.''',['ottazziwarhurst2011contact','guilleminquillensternberg1967infinite'],match='exact',reconstruction='The complex ground field and finite-dimensional input are indispensable; the scan does not settle the historical attribution.')
item(457,'P000826','remark','An infinite-dimensional input and a Poisson-algebra suggestion',r'''An example takes \(\mathfrak g_0=W_n\), \(\mathfrak g_{-1}=\mathbb C[x_1,\ldots,x_n]\), and says the prolongation is scarcely different from Hamiltonian fields, with a Poisson bracket.''',r'''Polynomial vector fields act on the polynomial algebra by derivations, so an algebraic prolongation recipe can be posed with this infinite-dimensional input. It lies outside the finite-dimensional rank-one criterion just stated. To identify its completion with a Poisson algebra requires the choice of algebraic versus continuous multilinear maps, grading, and the precise Poisson realization; none is supplied.''','F',r'''The sources checked for ordinary finite-dimensional prolongations do not fix these missing choices. The earlier Hamiltonian-vector-field cohomology problem GP-1975-21 is only thematic background, not the same question.''',verification='unresolved lead',match='none found',literal='not determinable',confidence='low')
item(457,'P000826-P000827','definition','Generalized Cartan matrices and symmetrizability',r'''A has diagonal2, nonpositive integer off-diagonal entries with simultaneous zeros, and can be made symmetric by \(n_{\alpha\beta}\mapsto n_{\alpha\beta}\lambda_\alpha/\lambda_\beta\). An invariant form is then mentioned.''',r'''A generalized Cartan matrix has these integrality and sign conditions. It is symmetrizable if a positive diagonal D makes DA symmetric; equivalently a positive diagonal similarity makes A symmetric, with D having entries \(\lambda_\alpha^2\) in the notebook's convention. The associated symmetrizable Kac--Moody algebra admits the standard invariant symmetric form, with the usual qualification on its center or full Cartan realization.''','E',r'''These matrices, invariant forms and contragredient constructions are treated in \citet[Chapter~II, Sections~1--4]{kac1968graded}.''',['kac1968graded'],match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Positive real scaling factors and the distinction between similarity and left symmetrization are stated explicitly.')
item(457,'P000827','construction','Chevalley-Serre generators and an index mismatch',r'''The notes use \([h_\beta,e_\alpha]=n_{\alpha\beta}e_\alpha\), \([e_\alpha,f_\alpha]=2h_\alpha\), and \((\operatorname{ad}e_\alpha)^{1-n_{\alpha\beta}}e_\beta=0\), with analogous f-relations.''',r'''With the recorded transposed convention for the Cartan action, the Serre exponent is \(1-n_{\beta\alpha}\), for \(\alpha\ne\beta\). The factor2 in \([e_\alpha,f_\alpha]=2h_\alpha\) is a harmless rescaling of one root generator in characteristic zero. For singular A, the presentation by only these3N generators describes the derived Kac--Moody algebra; the full standard algebra has an enlarged Cartan subalgebra.''','E',r'''The contragredient presentation and its reduced quotient are part of \citet[Chapter~II]{kac1968graded}. The index repair follows by comparing the root-string exponent with the actual action of \(h_\alpha\) on \(e_\beta\), and matters for the nonsymmetric rank-two example on the next page.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The scan confirms both the factor2 and the inconsistent index order; neither is silently re-transcribed.')
item(457,'P000827-P000828','definition','Root-lattice and height gradings',r'''The fine grading has degrees \(-\alpha,0,\alpha\) on \(f_\alpha,h_\alpha,e_\alpha\), and the coarse grading degrees \(-1,0,1\). Nonzero degrees with a nonzero component are roots; nonnegative simple-root combinations are called positive.''',r'''The homogeneous defining relations give a grading by the free root lattice \(Q=\bigoplus_i\mathbb Z\alpha_i\); composing with height gives the integer grading. A root is a nonzero \(\gamma\in Q\) with nonzero root space, and a positive root lies in \(Q_+\setminus\{0\}\). Not every nonnegative integral combination is a root.''','E',r'''These conventions are developed in \citet[Chapter~II]{kac1968graded}.''',['kac1968graded'],match='exact',reconstruction='The zero combination is excluded and membership in the actual root support is retained.')
item(457,'P000827','claim','Finite, affine, and indefinite growth',r'''Positive definite A gives a finite-dimensional algebra; semidefinite singular A gives growth1 with uniformly bounded graded dimensions; a negative eigenvalue is said to give exponential growth. The type-A chain matrix gives sl(n+1).''',r'''For an indecomposable symmetrizable generalized Cartan matrix, positivity of its symmetric form characterizes finite type, and positive semidefiniteness with a null direction characterizes affine type; the latter has bounded height-graded dimensions. The indefinite case has infinite growth in Kac's finite-growth classification. The stronger quantitative exponential statement is not inferred merely from the phrase infinite growth in that source. The displayed n-by-n tridiagonal matrix is type \(A_n\), giving \(\mathfrak{sl}_{n+1}\).''','E',r'''The finite-growth alternatives are read in \citet[Chapter~II, Theorem~1 and Section~7]{kac1968graded}. A theorem-level check of the notebook's stronger exponential wording was not completed; the fate match is therefore partial.''',['kac1968graded'],match='partial',confidence='medium',reconstruction='The symmetric form, indecomposability and meaning of growth are supplied; no false equivalence between superpolynomial and exponential growth is made.')
item(457,'P000828','construction','The center from the kernel of the Cartan matrix',r'''If \(\sum_\beta k_\beta n_{\alpha\beta}=0\), then \(\sum_\beta k_\beta h_\beta\) is central. Two rank-two affine matrices are displayed.''',r'''In the notebook's index convention, the displayed kernel condition makes the Cartan element commute with every e,f,h generator, hence central. The matrices \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}2&-2\\-2&2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) and \(\left(\begin{smallmatrix}2&-1\\-4&2\end{smallmatrix}\right)\) are the affine types \(A_1^{(1)}\) and \(A_2^{(2)}\), up to transposition. Their derived algebras have a one-dimensional center.''','E',r'''These affine rank-two types occur in the tables and realizations of \citet[Chapter~II, Sections~5--7]{kac1968graded}. The centrality check is also immediate from the displayed relations.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='correct',match='exact',reconstruction='The second matrix is not symmetric; symmetric counterpart in the translation means its transpose here.')
item(457,'P000828','construction','Untwisted loop algebras and the missing central quotient',r'''The first affine rank-two matrix is said to give \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\otimes\mathbb C[t,t^{-1}]\); more generally use any finite-dimensional g, with the extra generators obtained from the longest root vectors times \(t^{\pm1}\).''',r'''For finite-dimensional simple complex g, the center quotient of the derived untwisted affine algebra is its loop algebra \(g\otimes\mathbb C[t,t^{-1}]\). The derived affine algebra is its central extension; the full affine algebra adds the degree derivation. The extra simple-root generators use the highest root and its negative, rather than an unspecified longest root. Passing to the loop quotient kills the central linear combination of h's recorded immediately above.''','E',r'''The distinction between contragredient and reduced algebras, and their loop realizations, is explicit in \citet[Chapter~II, Section~7]{kac1968graded}.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The finite-dimensional input must be simple for the single affine type assertion; semisimple inputs give direct sums.')
item(457,'P000829','construction','Twisted loop algebras from an involution',r'''For \(\tau^2=1\), decompose \(\mathfrak g=\mathfrak g_+\oplus\mathfrak g_-\) and take even powers with \(\mathfrak g_+\), odd with \(\mathfrak g_-\). The example is \(\mathfrak{sl}_3\), \(\tau(X)=-X^T\).''',r'''The parity eigenspace rule defines the fixed algebra \(L(\mathfrak g,\tau)=\bigoplus_{m\in\mathbb Z}\mathfrak g_{(-1)^m}t^m\), closed under brackets because eigenvalues multiply. For the nontrivial diagram involution of \(\mathfrak{sl}_3\), the corresponding central extension is twisted affine type \(A_2^{(2)}\). Again the displayed loop algebra itself is the center quotient.''','E',r'''This is among the twisted realizations in \citet[Chapter~II, Section~7]{kac1968graded}.''',['kac1968graded'],match='exact',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='An arbitrary involution gives a twisted-loop presentation, but its affine-type identification depends on its outer/diagram class.')
item(457,'P000829','construction','A one-dimensional graded bracket with period three',r'''There are generators \(\varepsilon_i\), \(i\in\mathbb Z\), and \([\varepsilon_i,\varepsilon_j]=a_{ij}\varepsilon_{i+j}\), where \(a_{ij}\in\{-1,0,1\}\) is congruent to \(j-i\pmod3\). It is called simple.''',r'''This bracket is the principal grading of the centerless loop algebra of \(\mathfrak{sl}_2\). For \([h,e]=2e,[h,f]=-2f,[e,f]=h\), one realization is \(\varepsilon_{3r}=\tfrac12ht^r\), \(\varepsilon_{3r+1}=et^r\), \(\varepsilon_{3r-1}=\tfrac12ft^r\). It is graded-simple in the intended sense; it is not simple as an ungraded Lie algebra, since \((t-1)\mathfrak{sl}_2[t,t^{-1}]\) is a nonzero proper ideal.''','E',r'''The distinction is already explicit in the definition of simple in \citet[Introduction]{kac1968graded}. The given basis realization verifies all nine residue cases directly; it identifies an old loop algebra, not a new classification result.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The normalization of e,f,h and the grading are supplied. The counterexample ideal is not homogeneous in this grading.')
item(457,'P000829','claim','The incomplete three-algebra and exhaustion statements',r'''The notes say there are three simple algebras with one generator in each integer degree, give only one bracket, and end: these two constructions exhaust everything up to grading.''',r'''No classification of all integer-graded Lie algebras follows without growth, irreducibility, generation and graded-simplicity assumptions. Kac's finite-growth theorem has a precise scope and produces reduced contragredient and Cartan families. The two missing one-dimensional brackets and the antecedent of these two constructions cannot be uniquely recovered from this page.''','F',r'''The scoped theorem is \citet[Theorem~4]{kac1968graded}; its hypotheses are substantially more specific than the notebook's final sentence. The broader one-dimensional-component classification was not identified as an exact theorem in the supplied wording.''',['kac1968graded'],literal='not determinable',match='partial',confidence='low',reconstruction='The established finite-growth classification is separated from the incomplete last announcement.')
item(457,'P000830','construction','Hermitian matrices, spinors and the Lorentz cover',r'''A real four-vector is the Hermitian matrix \(X=x^0I+x^i\sigma_i\); its determinant is the Lorentz metric, and \(X\mapsto A^*XA\) gives the Lorentz action. Two Weyl spinor representations are used.''',r'''The determinant is \((x^0)^2-(x^1)^2-(x^2)^2-(x^3)^2\). The action of \(SL(2,\mathbb C)\) on Hermitian2-by-2 matrices preserves it and induces the double cover of \(SO^+(1,3)\), with kernel \(\{I,-I\}\). Dotted and undotted Weyl indices transform in conjugate representations. The inverse vector-to-matrix map uses the trace pairing and a factor1/2, rather than an unqualified repeated Pauli-matrix contraction.''','E',r'''These conventions underlie \citet[Appendix]{haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries}. The determinant identity is also immediate from the matrix printed in the notebook.''',['haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries'],speaker='V. I. Ogievetsky',area='Mathematical physics',match='partial',reconstruction='The proper orthochronous component, kernel, conjugation convention and inverse normalization are made explicit.')
item(457,'P000830','definition','The real N=1 super-Poincare algebra',r'''Ten even generators P,L and four real spinorial odd generators satisfy \(\{Q_\alpha,\bar Q_{\dot\beta}\}=2\sigma^m_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_m\), \(\{Q,Q\}=\{\bar Q,\bar Q\}=0\), and \([P,Q]=0\); dimension is written(10,4).''',r'''Add the usual Poincare brackets and the Lorentz spinor action on Q and its conjugate. These relations define the four-dimensional real N=1 super-Poincare algebra of superdimension \(10\mid4\). The odd bracket is symmetric and must satisfy super-Jacobi; it is not an ordinary Lie bracket. Its kinematic algebra by itself is not a construction of a supergravity action or field equations.''','E',r'''This algebra and its allowed extensions are within \citet[Section~1, equations(1.1)--(1.7)]{haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries}, received15November1974 and published1975. Earlier occurrence1975-389.1--.7 is background on supergeometry, not the same indexed open problem.''',['haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries'],speaker='V. I. Ogievetsky',area='Mathematical physics',match='exact',reconstruction='Reality, conjugate indices, the even-odd brackets and the super-Jacobi condition complete the recorded relations.')
item(457,'P000830-P000831','construction','Extended supersymmetry and the role of internal indices',r'''With several spinors, \(\{Q_\alpha^i,\bar Q_{\dot\beta}^j\}=2\sigma^m_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_m\delta^{ij}\); i,j are internal-symmetry indices. The odd bracket is said to be unique.''',r'''With a positive Hermitian internal metric put into standard form, this is the translation-producing odd bracket for N supersymmetries. Lorentz covariance determines its form up to normalization in the N=1 case. For N greater than1, super-Jacobi permits antisymmetric central charges in \(\{Q_\alpha^i,Q_\beta^j\}=\varepsilon_{\alpha\beta}Z^{ij}\). Thus uniqueness requires a fixed even algebra and the explicit exclusion of central extensions.''','E',r'''Central charges and restrictions from internal symmetry are proved in \citet[Sections~1 and~3]{haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries}. The notebook records the zero-central-charge specialization.''',['haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries'],speaker='V. I. Ogievetsky',area='Mathematical physics',match='exact',reconstruction='The internal metric, N and the central-extension qualification are supplied; a colloquial particle/antiparticle slogan is not treated as an equation of states.')
item(457,'P000831','claim','Mixing space-time and internal symmetries and the no-go scope',r'''The notes say this is a nontrivial mixing impossible in ordinary Lie algebras, by a theorem.''',r'''The relevant no-go conclusion concerns continuous symmetries of a nontrivial relativistic S-matrix under specified assumptions, including suitable particle finiteness and regularity; it is not a theorem forbidding arbitrary semidirect products of ordinary Lie algebras. Allowing odd spinorial generators gives the supersymmetry extension. Massive and massless cases have different allowed enlargements.''','E',r'''The hypotheses and the extension of the Coleman--Mandula framework are explicit in \citet[Sections~1--2 and~5]{haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries}.''',['haaglopuszanskisohnius1975supersymmetries'],speaker='V. I. Ogievetsky',area='Mathematical physics',literal='false as written',match='partial',reconstruction='The statement is restricted to the physical symmetry setting of the theorem; no universal algebraic prohibition is endorsed.')
item(457,'P000831','construction','Superspace and the finite superfield expansion',r'''Adjoin \(\theta^\alpha,\bar\theta^{\dot\alpha}\), four anticommuting variables. A superfield expands through \(\theta^2\bar\theta^2\), and \(\varepsilon_{12}=-\varepsilon_{21}=1\) is the spinor metric.''',r'''The local superfunction algebra is the ordinary space-time function algebra tensored with an exterior algebra on four odd generators. Its degrees have dimensions \(1,4,6,4,1\), so a general scalar superfield has16 coefficient functions, eight even and eight odd monomials. For an even superfield, spinor coefficient fields have odd parity. The mixed quadratic term is \(\theta\sigma^m\bar\theta\,v_m(x)\), with a vector index; the antisymmetric epsilon tensor raises and lowers spinor indices.''','E',r'''The superspace/superfield construction predates this1979 meeting: \citet{salamstrathdee1975superfields}, submitted12June1974 and published15March1975. Publisher abstract and metadata were checked; the precise component notation here is reconstructed from the exterior algebra, not read from that full paper.''',['salamstrathdee1975superfields'],speaker='V. I. Ogievetsky',area='Mathematical physics',verification='publisher metadata checked',match='partial',reconstruction='The ellipsis includes the cubic spinor terms; dotted conjugate indices and the vector index repair the compressed expression.')
item(457,'P000831','construction','Odd differential operators realizing supertranslations',r'''With \(P_m=-i\partial_m\), the notes write \(Q_\alpha=-i\partial_{\theta^\alpha}-(\sigma^m\bar\theta)_\alpha\partial_m\) and \(\bar Q_{\dot\alpha}=i\bar\partial_{\dot\alpha}+(\theta\sigma^m)_{\dot\alpha}\partial_m\), after an obscured cancellation.''',r'''For left Grassmann derivatives these recorded signs give \(\{Q_\alpha,\bar Q_{\dot\beta}\}=-2i\sigma^m_{\alpha\dot\beta}\partial_m=2\sigma^m_{\alpha\dot\beta}P_m\), and the same-chirality anticommutators vanish. The displayed orbital \(L_{mn}=-i(x_m\partial_n-x_n\partial_m)\) must be supplemented by its action on the spinor coordinates to realize the whole super-Poincare algebra on superfields.''','E',r'''Differential superspace realizations are the subject of \citet{salamstrathdee1975superfields}; only its abstract/metadata were available. The notebook signs are independently checked algebraically here using the Grassmann derivative rule, without guessing the cancelled mark.''',['salamstrathdee1975superfields'],speaker='V. I. Ogievetsky',area='Mathematical physics',verification='indirect',match='partial',literal='correct with missing hypotheses',reconstruction='Left derivatives, the full Lorentz action and the remaining illegible mark are distinguished. The cancellation does not prevent checking the surviving supertranslation formula.')
